\chapter{Superconducting Magnetics}

\section{Overview}
The superconducting magnet array of the Matter Jet Mk-30 is responsible for
generating the ultra-high axial magnetic field required for relativistic jet
collimation. This chapter develops the physics of REBCO superconductors,
cryogenic stability, magnetic nozzle geometry, quench dynamics, and the
electromagnetic constraints governing the 1450~T confinement field.

\section{High-Temperature Superconductors}
The Mk-30 employs Rare-Earth Barium Copper Oxide (REBCO) tapes, which exhibit
high critical current density $J_c$ under strong magnetic fields.

The critical current density is modeled as
\begin{equation}
J_c(T,B) = J_{c0} \exp\left( -\frac{B}{B_0} \right)
\exp\left( -\frac{T}{T_0} \right),
\end{equation}
where:
\begin{itemize}
\item $J_{c0}$ is the zero-field critical current density,
\item $B_0$ is the magnetic field decay constant,
\item $T_0$ is the thermal decay constant.
\end{itemize}

At 4.2~K and 1450~T, REBCO tapes maintain sufficient $J_c$ for Mk-30 operation.

\section{Cryogenic Envelope}
The superconducting array is cooled by a multi-stage liquid helium (LHe)
cryogenic system.

The heat load on the cryogenic envelope is
\begin{equation}
Q_{\text{cryo}} = \dot{m}_{\text{LHe}} c_p \Delta T,
\end{equation}
where $\Delta T$ is the temperature rise across the cryogenic jacket.

The Mk-30 maintains:
\[
T_{\text{cryo}} = 4.2~\text{K}.
\]

\section{Magnetic Field Generation}
The axial magnetic field generated by the superconducting coil is
\begin{equation}
B_z = \mu_0 n I,
\end{equation}
where:
\begin{itemize}
\item $n$ is turn density,
\item $I$ is current.
\end{itemize}

The Mk-30 achieves:
\[
B_z = 1450~\text{T}.
\]

The radial gradient is
\[
\frac{\partial B}{\partial r} = 8.12 \times 10^3~\text{T/m}.
\]

\section{Magnetic Nozzle Geometry}
The magnetic nozzle consists of:
\begin{itemize}
\item a throat radius $r_0$,
\item confinement length $L$,
\item axial field $B_z$,
\item radial gradient $\partial B / \partial r$.
\end{itemize}

The nozzle geometry is optimized to minimize plasma-wall interaction and maximize
Poynting flux conversion.

\section{Lorentz Force and Plasma Confinement}
The Lorentz force density is
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{f}_{\text{EM}} = \mathbf{J} \times \mathbf{B}.
\end{equation}

The Mk-30 confinement field produces:
\[
|\mathbf{f}_{\text{EM}}| \approx 10^{12}~\text{N/m}^3.
\]

This force confines the relativistic plasma and prevents nozzle erosion.

\section{Quench Dynamics}
A quench occurs when a portion of the superconducting coil transitions to the
normal resistive state.

The quench propagation velocity is
\begin{equation}
v_q = \sqrt{\frac{k}{\rho c_p}},
\end{equation}
where:
\begin{itemize}
\item $k$ is thermal conductivity,
\item $\rho$ is density,
\item $c_p$ is specific heat.
\end{itemize}

The Mk-30 interlock system detects quench precursors within
\[
\tau_{\text{detect}} = 0.02~\mu\text{s}.
\]

\section{Magnetic Stress and Structural Limits}
The magnetic pressure is
\begin{equation}
p_B = \frac{B^2}{2\mu_0}.
\end{equation}

For $B = 1450$~T,
\[
p_B \approx 1.05 \times 10^{12}~\text{Pa}.
\]

The coil structure must withstand this pressure without deformation.

The stainless steel backing provides:
\[
\sigma_{\text{yield}} \approx 1.8 \times 10^9~\text{Pa},
\]
which is sufficient when combined with geometric reinforcement.

\section{Electromagnetic Energy Density}
The electromagnetic energy density is
\begin{equation}
u_{\text{EM}} = \frac{B^2}{2\mu_0}.
\end{equation}

For the Mk-30,
\[
u_{\text{EM}} \approx 1.05 \times 10^{12}~\text{J/m}^3.
\]

This energy density drives relativistic plasma acceleration.

\section{Magnetic Field Stability}
Field stability requires:
\begin{itemize}
\item constant current supply,
\item cryogenic temperature stability,
\item suppression of flux creep,
\item rapid quench detection.
\end{itemize}

The Mk-30 achieves stability through:
\begin{itemize}
\item FPGA interlock logic,
\item redundant cryogenic channels,
\item real-time magnetic field monitoring,
\item zero-trust telemetry.
\end{itemize}

\section{Summary}
The superconducting magnet array of the Mk-30 provides:
\begin{itemize}
\item 1450~T axial confinement,
\item strong radial gradients,
\item cryogenic stability at 4.2~K,
\item rapid quench detection,
\item structural resilience under magnetic pressure.
\end{itemize}

These properties enable the relativistic jet collimation and acceleration
developed in earlier chapters. The next chapter addresses the high-flux
thermodynamics of the cooling and radiator systems.

